              "bbox": [
                0.687,
                0.289,
                0.892,
                0.512
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.481,
            0.698,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You all may be familiar with this unique clay pot which is called Surahi (Fig. 8.8). In summers, surahi is used to keep the water cold."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.717,
            0.53,
            0.857,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 8.8: A surahi"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.55,
            0.567,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8.7 How do Clouds give us Rain?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.579,
            0.478,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Condensation plays a significant"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.601,
            0.451,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "role in the process of bringing evaporated water back to the Earth’s surface. How does this happen? When air moves higher above the Earth’s surface, it becomes cooler and cooler. At certain heights, the air gets so cool that the water vapour in it turns into droplets which are generally formed"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.464,
            0.605,
            0.758,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why does air containing water vapour go up in the atmosphere (thin layer of air that surrounds the Earth)?"
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.466,
            0.688,
            0.76,
            0.786
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As we know, gas balloons containing lighter gases go up in the air. Similarly, water vapour is lighter than air, causing it to rise."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.789,
            0.665,
            0.854,
            0.716
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 14,
              "bbox": [
                0.789,
                0.665,
                0.854,
                0.716
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_14_image_14.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.776,
            0.728,
            0.868,
            0.77
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do you know?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.804,
            0.746,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "around dust particles. These small droplets float in the air and form clouds. Many droplets join together to form bigger drops of water. Some drops get so heavy that they start falling. These falling water drops are what we call rain."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "aside_text",
